<speak>
<p>Fix a prime p and a root of unity eta whose order is coprime to p. Let A be the integer ring generated by eta, localized at one prime above p: denominators outside that prime are allowed. We use the cyclotomic facts supplied by Lemma two point one. The coefficient ring is unramified at p, with integer valuation normalized by v of p equals one.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Suppose F times K equals p to the e times b on a cycle of length p to the e, with e at least one and b a unit. For a primitive p-th root rho, our targets are the ratios F of rho over F of one, and K of rho over K of one. Both denominators are nonzero, but may have zero residue. Although rho reduces to one, dividing two zero residues would not justify either ratio.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>The local ring A with rho adjoined has maximal ideal generated by rho minus one. A nonzero residue therefore means a unit. First take e equal to one. The nonnegative integer valuations of F of one and K of one add to one. At least one is zero; suppose F of one is a unit. Its ratio safely reduces to one. The two ratios multiply to one, since the evaluated products at rho and at one both equal p b. The other ratio is the inverse, also a unit of residue one.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>For e at least two, evaluate at a primitive p to the e-th root xi. The valuations of F of xi and K of xi are nonnegative and add to e. They may be fractional, but one must be at least one. Relabel the factors so that it is F of xi.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Divide F by the monic cyclotomic polynomial: F equals Q times Phi, plus R, with degree of R less than D. Here D is the product of p minus one and p raised to the exponent e minus one. At xi, Phi vanishes, so R of xi has valuation at least one. The first D powers of xi form an A basis. The local maximal ideal is generated by xi minus one. The supplied input makes every remainder coefficient divisible by p. This is an arithmetic basis fact, not complex magnitude.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Now project to the cycle of length p to the e minus one; call this projection q. Each of the p terms of Phi becomes one, so q of Phi equals p. Both parts of q of F are divisible by p. Define F prime as q of F divided by p, and K prime as q of K. Their product is p to the e minus one times b. Cancelling p is valid in the free A module. This is exact division before reducing residues.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Both inputs, one and rho, satisfy the shorter cycle relation. Projection changes neither evaluation. Dividing F by p cancels from numerator and denominator, so F prime of rho over F prime of one equals F of rho over F of one. The ratio for K is unchanged too.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Repeat the shortening. The cycle exponent and scalar exponent decrease together, while both ratios remain unchanged. We may divide different factors at different steps. Stop when the exponent reaches one. The base case then proves that both original ratios are units of residue one.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Apply this to f times f star equals u squared, with u odd and greater than one. Fix p dividing u. Its cycle component has order p to the e, where e is exactly the exponent of p in u squared. Evaluate the other prime components first and localize their coefficient ring. Their root orders are coprime to p. Write b as u squared divided by p to the e; it is a unit here. The remaining product is p to the e times b. The exponents match, so our comparison applies.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>For a subset S not containing p, the ratio f at zeta sub S union p, divided by f at zeta sub S, is a unit of residue one at every prime above p. Its inverse has the same property. This conclusion belongs to the ratios, not to the individual values. These comparisons will control the denominators in the alternating product.<break time="1000ms"/></p>
</speak>
